        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill for sprinting?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For dedicated sprint training, a curved manual trainer such as the SOLE C80, because it has no motor lag and no top speed. If you also want to run easy miles and walk on incline, a motorised treadmill with a high top speed, such as the NordicTrack Commercial 2450 at 14 mph, is the better all-rounder.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How fast can you sprint on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most home treadmills stop at 12 mph, a 5:00 mile pace. A few, including the NordicTrack Commercial 2450, reach 14 mph. Curved manual trainers have no electronic limit, so the belt moves as fast as you can drive it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are curved treadmills better for sprinting?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For short, maximal efforts, usually yes. The belt speeds up and slows down as you do, with no motor to ramp up, and there is no top speed. They are less suited to steady running and walking, have no incline, and cost around $1,600 to $2,500.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What motor do you need for sprinting on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Look for a continuous-duty rating of at least 3 CHP, and more if you are heavy or sprint often. The motorised picks here are rated 3.5 and 4.25 CHP. A bigger continuous rating usually means the belt slows less under each hard foot strike.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it safe to sprint on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, with care. On a motorised treadmill, clip on the safety key, never jump on or off a belt at speed, and keep about 2 m clear behind the deck. Incline sprints at a moderate speed carry less fall risk than flat sprints near top speed.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long should a treadmill deck be for sprinting?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At least 60 in. Stride length grows with speed, and a short deck leaves little margin if you drift back. The motorised picks here have 60 in. belts, and the SOLE C80's curved deck is 62 in.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
